From adf5479604052eef8b87c1763ca63ac68bb71412 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 07:08:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] reims-vgpu: arm a tracked set from the main loop, not from
 the next doorbell

A set armed only inside reims_vgpu_dirty_harvest, which ran only from a guest
doorbell write that followed a generation read. A set tracked while the guest was
quiet, or inside one long drain tranche (which drains one doorbell's work), read
generation 0 for as long as the guest stayed away: up to 8.24 s measured, the
window re-gathered on every bind.

track() and every generation read that comes back 0 now schedule a main-loop
bottom half that runs the same harvest under the BQL, and reschedules itself
while a set still owes a second harvest (the first region of a boot). The
harvest itself is unchanged, so arming still happens at a harvest after logging
was active and still absorbs the bits that predate the set.

A baseline cannot be taken inside track(): it is called from the drain thread,
which must not take the BQL the dirty-log sync needs, and a generation made
readable without absorbing stale bits would report every pre-existing bit as a
guest store to guest_written_pages, which the writeback rail answers by
dropping the GPU's store for those pages.

reims-vgpu-dirty-test/ drives the real adapter source against a model of the
hypervisor bitmap: directed arming cases, and an exhaustive check of every
interleaving of guest writes, harvests, reads, copies and vouches. Four mutants,
including the synchronous baseline, must fail it.
